✅✅ NoSQL ✅✅
======================== Why NoSQL Handles Scaling Better ========================

NoSQL assumes:
network failures are normal
distributed systems are default
joins are expensive

Comparison:
Problem	                    RDBMS     	NoSQL
Horizontal Scaling	        Hard	      Built-in
Global Distribution	        Complex    	Native
Massive Writes	            Limited   	High Throughput

======================== Why SQL Databases Avoid Automatic Sharding ========================

Traditional SQL databases are designed around:
single-node ACID
strong consistency
local joins

Automatic sharding complicates:
joins
transactions
consistency guarantees

So most SQL systems expect:
Application manages sharding

======================== NoSQL Philosophy ========================

NoSQL systems are built for:
massive scale
distributed systems
flexible schema
horizontal scaling

They encourage:
Denormalization

Store related data together:
avoid joins
avoid cross-shard fetches
one read from one machine

======================== CAP Theorem ========================
Consistency
Every read gets latest write (or error).

Availability
Every request gets a response.

Partition Tolerance
System continues despite network failures.

In distributed systems:
Partition tolerance is mandatory
So systems usually choose tradeoffs between:
CP or AP

Common distributed problems:
node failures
replication lag
partial availability

======================== Horizontal Scaling in NoSQL ========================

RDBMS:
Scale Up

NoSQL:
Scale Out

Add more nodes instead of bigger machines.
NoSQL databases automatically manage:

partitioning
replication
node routing

Often using:
Consistent Hashing
which helps while adding/removing nodes.

Each node has:
CPU
memory
disk

No central bottleneck.

======================== Node in NoSQL ========================

SQL thinking:
Tables
Rows
Joins
One big database

NoSQL thinking:
Keys
Partitions
Nodes
Clusters

A node:
One machine or DB instance

Example:
VM
server
cloud instance

======================== Partition ========================

A partition:
Logical chunk of data
decided by:
key
hash
range

Not:
a table
a machine

Analogy:
Big book -> chapters
Each chapter -> partition

Partitions live on nodes.

======================== Partition Placement ========================
1. One Partition Per Node
Node A -> Partition 1
Node B -> Partition 2

2. Multiple Partitions Per Node
Node A -> P1, P2, P3

3. Replicated Partitions
Partition 1:
Primary -> Node A
Replica -> Node B

Used for:
fault tolerance
high availability
read scaling

======================== Why NoSQL Partitioning is Different from SQL Sharding ========================
SQL Sharding

In SQL systems:

each shard is a separate database
application decides shard routing

Example:

Shard1 -> users 1-250k
Shard2 -> users 250k-500k

Problems:

app must know shard logic
adding new DBs is painful
rebalancing requires app changes
shard management becomes operationally complex
NoSQL Partitioning

In NoSQL:

key -> hash(key) -> partition -> node

Application simply does:

get(userId)

Database automatically handles:

partition selection
node routing
replication
failover

App does not know where data lives.

======================== Why Partitions Exist ========================

Why not directly map data to nodes?

Because:

Nodes change frequently
Data distribution should stay stable

Nodes:

fail
get added/removed
become slow

Partitions sit between:

Data <-> Nodes

This allows independent:

movement
balancing
replication

======================== Problems Partitions Solve ========================
1. Easier Rebalancing
Without partitions:
Node A -> 50%
Node B -> 50%

Add Node C:
Move huge amount of data manually

With partitions:
100 partitions total
Only some partitions move to new node.
Much cheaper rebalancing.

2. Hot Partition Handling
Example:
user:1 -> millions of requests

Partitioning allows DB to:
isolate hot data
rebalance independently
move partitions separately

3. Failure Recovery
If:
Node B dies
DB can:
promote replica partitions
reassign ownership
recover automatically

Without partitions, entire shard handling becomes harder.

======================== Suitcase Analogy ========================

Without partitions:
Node = suitcase
Data = clothes

Adding new suitcase:
Move clothes manually

With partitions:
Partitions = small bags
Nodes = suitcases

Adding new node:
Move only few bags

======================== Why SQL Cannot Easily Use Consistent Hashing ========================

SQL databases are built around:
strong ACID transactions
joins
foreign keys
local consistency

These assume:
Data and locks are local

Consistent hashing assumes:
data can live anywhere
nodes can fail anytime
network partitions happen

These ideas conflict with traditional SQL guarantees.
That is why:

NoSQL prioritizes scalability/distribution
SQL prioritizes consistency/transactions
